<article class="env-lemma" id="EDEED9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/EDEED9">Lemma <span data-tag="EDEED9">3.2.5.11</span>.</a> Let $\calI $ be a category and let $\alpha : f \rightarrow f'$ be a natural transformation of functors $f,f': \calC \rightarrow \sSet $. </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
Suppose that, for each $I \in \calC $, the map $\alpha (I): f(I) \rightarrow f'(I)$ is an inner fibration of simplicial sets. Then the induced map $\Nerve _{f}(\calC ) \rightarrow \Nerve _{f'}(\calC )$ is an inner fibration. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
Suppose that, for each $I \in \calI $, the simplicial set $f(I)$ is an $\infty $-category. Then $\Nerve _{f}(\calC )$ is an $\infty $-category. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
Suppose that, for each $I \in \calC $, the map $\alpha (I): f(I) \rightarrow f'(I)$ is a categorical fibration of $\infty $-categories. Then the induced map $\Nerve _{f}(\calC ) \rightarrow \Nerve _{f'}(\calC )$ is a categorical fibration of $\infty $-categories. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider a commutative diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \Lambda ^ n_ i \ar [r] \ar @{^{(}->}[d] &amp;  \Nerve _{f}(\calI ) \ar [d]^{p} \\ \Delta ^ n \ar @{-->}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  \Nerve _{f'}(\calC ), } \]
</div>
    <p>
       and let $I$ be the image of $\{ n\}  \subseteq \Delta ^ n$ under the bottom map. If $0 \leq i < n$, then the lifting problem depicted in the diagram above is equivalent to the existence of a dotted arrow in an associated diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \Lambda ^ n_ i \ar [r]^{g} \ar @{^{(}->}[d] &amp;  f(I) \ar [d]^{\alpha (I)} \\ \Delta ^ n \ar @{-->}[ur] \ar [r] &amp;  f'(I)} \]
</div>
    <p>
       If $\alpha (I)$ is an inner fibration and $0 < i < n$, then we conclude that this lifting problem admits a solution. This proves $(1)$. To prove $(2)$, we apply $(1)$ in the special case where $f'$ is the constant functor taking the value $\Delta ^0$. It follows that $\Nerve _{f}(\calC ) \rightarrow \Nerve (\calC )$ is an inner fibration, so that $\Nerve _{f}(\calC )$ is an $\infty $-category. 
    <p>
      We now prove $(3)$. According to Corollary <a href="/tag/16F445" data-tag="16F445">2.4.6.5</a>, an inner fibration $\calD \rightarrow \calE $ of $\infty $-categories is a categorical fibration if and only if the following condition is satisfied: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(\ast )$"><p>
For every equivalence $e: E \rightarrow E'$ in $\calE $, and every object $D \in \calD $ lifting $E$, there exists an equivalence $\overline{e}: D \rightarrow D'$ in $\calD $ lifting $e$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
    <p>
      We can identify equivalences in $\Nerve _{f'}(\calC )$ with triples $(g: I \rightarrow I', X, e: X' \rightarrow Y)$ where $g$ is an isomorphism in $\calC $, $X$ is an object of $f'(I)$, $X'$ is the image of $X$ in $f'(I')$, and $e: X' \rightarrow Y$ is an equivalence in $f'(I')$. Given a lifting $\overline{X}$ of $X$ to $f(I)$, we can apply the assumption that $\alpha (I')$ is a categorical fibration (and Corollary <a href="/tag/16F445" data-tag="16F445">2.4.6.5</a>) to lift $e$ to an equivalence $\overline{e}: \overline{X}' \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ in $f(I')$. This produces the desired equivalence $(g: I \rightarrow I', \overline{X}, \overline{e}: \overline{X}' \rightarrow \overline{Y})$ in $\Nerve _{f}(\calC )$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>